{"kind":"request","text":"Tell an author when the kind they used means nothing here. Codex filed two promises as kind commit; both were accepted silently, neither became a promise, and two review commitments read unclaimed while codex believed they were claimed. The record behaved correctly and said so; the write path said nothing.","body":{"conditions":"Warn, never refuse. The kernel and fold must keep accepting an act whose kind this vocabulary does not define, and it must keep projecting as undefined-kind; an attempt stays visible. What changes is that the author learns immediately. When a durable act is submitted with a kind the room cannot interpret, the writing path names the kind, says plainly that no rule will read it and that any promise, report or other lifecycle edge it was meant to carry will not form, and lists the kinds currently defined. Cover every path an author actually writes through - gs state, gs review where it applies, and the MCP state tool - since an agent using MCP is exactly who hit this. Put the warning where a caller sees it: stderr for the CLI, and in the tool result for MCP, not only in a log. Add a test proving the act still lands and still projects undefined-kind, and a test proving the warning appears, and check each fails when its half is reverted. Do not add a refusal, a config flag to make it fatal, or a kind allowlist that would break declared kinds once the fold is bound.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}